window.COURSE = {"title": "Quantum Machine Learning", "chapter": {"id": "classical-information", "title": "Classical Systems and Information", "number": 1, "part": "Part I · Quantum computing", "body_file": "chapters/classical-information/overview.html", "body": "<p class=\"lead\">What does it mean to describe a system mathematically? Before introducing qubits, we build a complete classical model: states as labels, uncertainty as probability vectors, operations as stochastic matrices, and sequences as matrix products.</p>\n<p>This chapter follows the systems-first approach in the lecture slides. The theory, proofs and worked examples are the main reading path. The small experiments help you test predictions; each remains understandable through the accompanying text and equations.</p>\n<aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>Before you begin</strong><p>Basic arithmetic and elementary algebra are enough to start. We explain the column-vector convention and include a matrix-multiplication refresher in Lesson 1.4. No prior quantum physics or Qiskit experience is required.</p></aside>\n<section id=\"goals\"><h2>By the end of this chapter</h2><ul><li>Choose a finite state set and interpret basis vectors in a stated order.</li><li>Distinguish model probabilities, empirical frequencies, and conditional probabilities.</li><li>Construct a stochastic matrix from conditional output rules and derive <span>\\(p'=Mp\\)</span>.</li><li>Compose operations in the right order and prove that their product remains stochastic.</li></ul></section>\n<section id=\"sequence\"><h2>Lesson sequence</h2><ol class=\"chapter-lessons\"><li><a href=\"#classical-states\">Finite systems, states and basis labels</a><p>Start with a bit, switch and die. Separate a state label, a coordinate vector and a binary encoding.</p></li><li><a href=\"#classical-probability\">Probability distributions and repeated observations</a><p>Describe uncertainty, interpret finite samples and derive conditional probabilities.</p></li><li><a href=\"#classical-operations\">Deterministic and stochastic operations</a><p>Build operation matrices, derive their action, and distinguish normalization from reversibility.</p></li><li><a href=\"#classical-composition\">Composing operations</a><p>Track intermediate states, work a noncommuting example and prove stochastic closure.</p></li></ol></section>\n<section id=\"study\"><h2>How to study the chapter</h2><p>Read each explanation and work the calculation before changing a control. The embedded checks give feedback for each answer. End-of-lesson exercises include hints and worked reasoning. The recorded check count is a browser-local study aid, not a mastery score or a restriction on navigation.</p><p>The programming companion follows all four lessons using Python and NumPy. Classical probability does not need a quantum-computing library; Qiskit will enter when quantum circuits are introduced. You can read the chapter fully without running the notebook.</p><p><a class=\"button\" href=\"#classical-states\">Begin Lesson 1.1</a> <a href=\"https://colab.research.google.com/github/Ashutosh-Bhatia/quantum-machine-learning/blob/main/notebooks/00-classical-information.ipynb\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open the programming companion in Colab ↗</a></p></section>\n<section id=\"review\"><h2>Review status and source material</h2><p>These are newly expanded lecture notes, ready for instructor review. The three academic figures are explicitly marked as drafts so their notation, clarity and presentation can be refined from feedback. The later Part I chapters are planned; the earlier short prototype examples are available separately in the archive.</p><p>The primary source is <cite>Lecture Slides – 1 to 7</cite>, PDF pages 3–13, supported by the introductory July 26 lecture’s systems and probability explanations. Lesson-level notes identify the relevant pages and the added mathematical exposition. Raw classroom transcripts and source PDFs remain outside this public repository.</p></section>\n"}, "lessons": [{"id": "classical-states", "number": "1.1", "title": "Finite systems, states and basis labels", "part": "Part I · Chapter 1", "status": "Full lecture notes · instructor-review draft", "objectives": ["Choose a finite state set appropriate to a modelling question.", "Translate a definite state into a labelled standard basis vector.", "Distinguish coordinate dimension from binary storage size."], "body_file": "chapters/classical-information/1-states.html", "quizzes": [{"id": "states-order", "question": "If we reorder the coordinates, does the same switch setting necessarily keep the same column vector?", "options": ["Yes: coordinate order has no effect.", "No: the coordinates can change while the labelled setting stays the same.", "No: reordering physically changes the setting."], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["The position of the 1 depends on which coordinate corresponds to the setting.", "Correct. A vector must be interpreted with its declared basis order.", "Changing our coordinate convention does not operate on the physical device."]}, {"id": "states-capacity", "question": "What is the minimum fixed-length binary storage for five distinct labels?", "options": ["Two bits", "Three bits", "Five bits"], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["Two bits provide only four distinct strings.", "Correct. Three bits provide eight strings; three are unused in a five-label code.", "Five one-hot coordinates are one representation, but they are not the minimum number of storage bits."]}], "notebook": "00-classical-information.ipynb", "notebook_kind": "classical", "source": "Lecture Slides - 1 to 7.pdf, PDF pp.3–4. The introductory July 26 lecture transcript informs the systems-first explanation; classroom dialogue is paraphrased rather than published.", "prerequisites": "Basic arithmetic and elementary algebra. A matrix-multiplication refresher is included before composition.", "body": "<p class=\"lead\">Before asking what makes a quantum computer different, we need a language for describing a system, the information it stores, and the operations we can perform on it. We can develop that language using familiar classical systems.</p>\n<section id=\"systems\"><h2>1. A system and its possible states</h2>\n<p>Consider a device that we want to describe. A bit can have value 0 or 1. A die resting on a table can show one of six upper faces. A simplified fan switch can have three settings: off, low, or high. The first modelling decision is to choose which distinctions matter for our task.</p>\n<p>We call the collection of possible classical states the <strong>state set</strong>, denoted by \\(\\Sigma\\). For the examples above,</p>\n\\[\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{bit}}=\\{0,1\\},\\qquad \\Sigma_{\\mathrm{die}}=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\},\\qquad \\Sigma_{\\mathrm{switch}}=\\{\\mathrm{off},\\mathrm{low},\\mathrm{high}\\}.\\]\n<p>These are models of the devices. A real fan also has a temperature, a rotor speed, and many other properties. If the question is only which setting has been selected, the three-state description is sufficient. If we want to predict the motor's dynamics, it is not. A state description is always tied to the question and level of abstraction.</p>\n<aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>A state label is part of a model.</strong><p>The symbol “high” identifies one setting. It does not describe every microscopic detail of the fan, and renaming it does not change the physical device.</p></aside></section>\n<section id=\"labels\"><h2>2. Choosing labels and an order</h2>\n<p>Suppose our switch is definitely set to low. We can record that fact with the label “low.” But calculations become easier if all state descriptions have a common mathematical form. We therefore place the state labels in a fixed order and associate one coordinate with each label.</p>\n<p>Choose the order \\((\\mathrm{off},\\mathrm{low},\\mathrm{high})\\). The coordinate corresponding to the actual state will be 1; all other coordinates will be 0. This is a <strong>one-hot representation</strong>. The three coordinate vectors are</p>\n\\[e_{\\mathrm{off}}=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad e_{\\mathrm{low}}=\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad e_{\\mathrm{high}}=\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\0\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>The order is a convention, not an extra physical property. If we instead choose \\((\\mathrm{high},\\mathrm{off},\\mathrm{low})\\), the same setting low is represented by \\((0,0,1)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Its position changed because we reordered our coordinates. Its meaning did not change.</p>\n<p>This distinction will matter throughout the course: a coordinate vector can only be interpreted when its basis order is known. Later, multi-qubit circuit diagrams and software will introduce further ordering conventions. We start the habit of writing the order now.</p>\n<div data-quiz=\"states-order\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"basis\"><h2>3. Standard basis vectors and ket notation</h2>\n<p>For a state set with \\(d\\) labels, there are \\(d\\) standard coordinate vectors. The vector for label \\(a\\) has a 1 at position \\(a\\) and zeros elsewhere. These vectors form the <strong>standard basis</strong>: any \\(d\\)-component vector can be written uniquely as a weighted sum of them.</p>\n<p>For example, a vector \\(v=(v_{\\mathrm{off}},v_{\\mathrm{low}},v_{\\mathrm{high}})^{\\mathsf T}\\) can be written as</p>\n\\[v=v_{\\mathrm{off}}e_{\\mathrm{off}}+v_{\\mathrm{low}}e_{\\mathrm{low}}+v_{\\mathrm{high}}e_{\\mathrm{high}}.\\]\n<p>Why is this true? The first term contributes only to the off coordinate, the second only to low, and the third only to high. Adding them reconstructs each original component. The coefficients are unique because no other basis vector contributes to a given coordinate.</p>\n<p>We will also write \\(|a\\rangle\\) for the standard basis vector associated with a label \\(a\\). Read this symbol as “ket a.” In this chapter it is simply convenient notation for a column vector; using a ket does not, by itself, make the description quantum. For a bit in the order \\((0,1)\\),</p>\n\\[|0\\rangle=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad |1\\rangle=\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>A definite classical bit has one of these state labels. In the next lesson, we will use weighted sums of the basis vectors to describe uncertainty. The meaning of those weights will be stated explicitly: they will be classical probabilities.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/classical-information/state-representations.svg\" alt=\"Three switch labels are mapped to three-component one-hot columns and two-bit encodings. The binary string 11 is unused.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 1.</strong> A definite switch setting can have different mathematical encodings. Basis coordinates and binary storage have different dimensions. <span class=\"draft-label\">Draft figure for instructor review.</span></figcaption></figure></section>\n<section id=\"encoding\"><h2>4. Number of states versus number of bits</h2>\n<p>Our three-state switch used a three-component coordinate vector. Does it therefore require three binary storage bits? No. The length of the coordinate representation and the number of bits used to encode a label are different quantities.</p>\n<p>One bit has two possible strings, 0 and 1. Two bits have four: 00, 01, 10, and 11. In general, \\(n\\) bits provide \\(2^n\\) distinct strings because each of the \\(n\\) positions has two choices. To assign a different binary string to every label in a \\(d\\)-state set, we need</p>\n\\[2^n\\ge d.\\]\n<p>The smallest nonnegative integer satisfying this condition is</p>\n\\[n=\\lceil\\log_2 d\\rceil.\\]\n<p>If logarithms are unfamiliar, use the equivalent counting question: how many times must we double 1 before reaching at least \\(d\\)? The ceiling symbol means round up to the next integer. A single-state system needs no information-bearing bit to distinguish among alternatives.</p>\n<h3>Worked example: encoding the switch</h3>\n<p>For \\(d=3\\), one bit is insufficient because \\(2&lt;3\\). Two bits suffice because \\(4\\ge3\\). One possible code is off ↦ 00, low ↦ 01, high ↦ 10. The string 11 is unused. We must decide what an application does if that unused code appears; it should not silently acquire a fourth physical meaning.</p>\n<p>A six-state die similarly needs at least three binary digits for a fixed-length encoding: two bits provide only four strings, while three provide eight. Two strings remain unused. Neither encoding says that the device occupies all its possible states at once. It says how many distinct labels the storage scheme can distinguish.</p>\n<div data-lab=\"states\"></div>\n<div data-quiz=\"states-capacity\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"practice\"><h2>5. Practice and worked reasoning</h2>\n<p>Attempt these before opening the hints. They check the distinction between a system, a coordinate representation, and a binary code.</p>\n<ol class=\"exercise-list\"><li><strong>A four-setting switch.</strong> Define a state set for off, low, medium, and high. Write the standard basis vector for medium in that order. How many binary digits suffice to encode the setting?\n<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Assign one coordinate to each label. For the storage question, count how many strings two bits provide.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The state set is \\(\\{\\mathrm{off},\\mathrm{low},\\mathrm{medium},\\mathrm{high}\\}\\). Medium has vector \\((0,0,1,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Since \\(2^2=4\\), two binary digits suffice. The coordinate vector has four entries even though the binary encoding uses only two digits.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Changing coordinate order.</strong> Rewrite the medium vector using the order (high, medium, low, off). Explain what changed.\n<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Find medium in the new list instead of moving the 1 by a memorized rule.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>Medium is now the second label, so its vector is \\((0,1,0,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\). The representation changed; the labelled setting did not. Comparing raw columns without their basis labels would be misleading.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Five messages.</strong> You need a fixed-length binary code for five messages. Give a possible code and count unused strings.\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>Two bits give four strings, which is too few. Three bits give eight. For example, use 000, 001, 010, 011, and 100 for the messages. Three strings remain unused. The message labels still form a five-element state set, with five-component one-hot basis vectors if we choose that representation.</p></details></li></ol>\n<aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>What to carry forward</strong><p>Choose a state set and declare its order. A basis vector describes one definite label; a binary encoding stores the label using bit strings. Next we will describe uncertainty over labels with a probability vector.</p></aside></section>\n"}, {"id": "classical-probability", "number": "1.2", "title": "Probability distributions and repeated observations", "part": "Part I · Chapter 1", "status": "Full lecture notes · instructor-review draft", "objectives": ["Validate a probability vector and calculate event probabilities.", "Separate a model probability from a finite empirical frequency.", "Calculate conditional probabilities and explain zero-probability boundaries."], "body_file": "chapters/classical-information/2-probability.html", "quizzes": [{"id": "prob-valid", "question": "Which vector is a complete valid distribution on a two-state set?", "options": ["(0.2, 0.3)ᵀ", "(1.2, −0.2)ᵀ", "(0.75, 0.25)ᵀ"], "answer": 2, "feedback": ["The entries sum to 0.5, so some probability is missing.", "A negative probability is invalid even when the entries sum to one.", "Correct. Both entries are nonnegative and sum to one."]}, {"id": "prob-frequency", "question": "A model has P(1)=0.25, but a 100-trial batch contains 28 ones. What follows?", "options": ["The exact probability must be 0.28.", "The observed frequency is 0.28; it can differ from the model probability.", "The model has been proved false."], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["A finite frequency is an estimate, not a proof of the exact probability.", "Correct. Interpreting the size of the discrepancy also requires the sampling assumptions and an uncertainty analysis.", "This observation alone does not prove the model false. Finite samples fluctuate."]}], "notebook": "00-classical-information.ipynb", "notebook_kind": "classical", "source": "Lecture Slides - 1 to 7.pdf, PDF pp.5–6, and the introductory lecture’s probability discussion. Event/conditional-probability examples and the indicator expectation derivation are new supporting exposition.", "prerequisites": "Basic arithmetic and elementary algebra. A matrix-multiplication refresher is included before composition.", "body": "<p class=\"lead\">A system can have a definite classical state even when we do not know which state it has. A probability distribution describes that uncertainty—or the outcomes of a random preparation—without changing the set of possible state labels.</p>\n<section id=\"distributions\"><h2>1. Probability distributions as column vectors</h2>\n<p>Let \\(\\Sigma=\\{a_1,\\ldots,a_d\\}\\) be a finite state set in a declared order. Write \\(p_j=\\Pr(X=a_j)\\), where \\(X\\) denotes the state label obtained in the experiment. Collect these numbers into a column:</p>\n\\[p=\\begin{pmatrix}p_1\\\\\\vdots\\\\p_d\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad p_j\\ge0,\\qquad \\sum_{j=1}^{d}p_j=1.\\]\n<p>The entries must be nonnegative because they represent probabilities. They sum to one because the labels are mutually exclusive and exhaustive: exactly one of the modelled outcomes occurs on a trial. A vector such as \\((0.2,0.3)^{\\mathsf T}\\) is not a complete distribution on a two-state set; its entries sum to 0.5. A vector such as \\((1.2,-0.2)^{\\mathsf T}\\) is also invalid, even though its sum is one.</p>\n<p>A <strong>point mass</strong> describes certainty about one label. For a bit, the distributions \\((1,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\) and \\((0,1)^{\\mathsf T}\\) coincide with the basis vectors from the previous lesson. Uncertainty allows other nonnegative normalized combinations. For example,</p>\n\\[p=\\frac34|0\\rangle+\\frac14|1\\rangle=\\begin{pmatrix}3/4\\\\1/4\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>Here the coefficients themselves are probabilities. They are not quantum amplitudes. Later the course will change the rules for state descriptions; we will then explain why squared magnitudes enter measurement probabilities. Applying that later rule to this classical vector would be a mistake.</p>\n<div data-quiz=\"prob-valid\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"events\"><h2>2. Events collect possible outcomes</h2>\n<p>An <strong>event</strong> is a subset of the state set. If a die's distribution in face order 1 through 6 is</p>\n\\[p=\\frac1{12}\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\2\\\\1\\\\3\\\\2\\\\3\\end{pmatrix},\\]\n<p>the event “the face is even” is \\(E=\\{2,4,6\\}\\). Since only one face is observed, these outcomes are mutually exclusive. Therefore,</p>\n\\[\\Pr(E)=p_2+p_4+p_6=\\frac{2+3+3}{12}=\\frac23.\\]\n<p>We add the probabilities of the relevant labels. We do not need to invent a new physical state called “even”; it is a question about a collection of outcomes. The complement \\(E^c\\) contains the outcomes not in \\(E\\), so \\(\\Pr(E^c)=1-\\Pr(E)\\).</p>\n<p>Care is needed when adding probabilities of events that overlap. For any two events,</p>\n\\[\\Pr(A\\cup B)=\\Pr(A)+\\Pr(B)-\\Pr(A\\cap B).\\]\n<p>The final term removes outcomes counted twice. For disjoint events it is zero. This is why simply adding the probabilities of individual distinct die faces was valid.</p></section>\n<section id=\"frequency\"><h2>3. A model probability is not a finite observed frequency</h2>\n<p>Suppose a preparation produces a bit with \\(\\Pr(X=1)=1/4\\). Repeat that preparation independently \\(N\\) times under the same conditions. If \\(N_1\\) outcomes are 1, the empirical frequency is</p>\n\\[\\widehat p_1=\\frac{N_1}{N}.\\]\n<p>The hat distinguishes an estimate from the underlying model probability. In 100 trials, 25 ones is the expected count. But 24, 28, or another nearby count can occur. An illustrative observation of 28 ones gives \\(\\widehat p_1=0.28\\), not proof that the preparation's true probability is exactly 0.28.</p>\n<h3>Why the expected count is \\(Np_1\\)</h3>\n<p>Define an indicator \\(I_t\\) for trial \\(t\\): it is 1 if that trial produces a 1 and 0 otherwise. Its expected value is \\(1\\cdot p_1+0\\cdot(1-p_1)=p_1\\). Because the total count is \\(N_1=I_1+\\cdots+I_N\\), linearity of expectation gives</p>\n\\[\\mathbb E[N_1]=\\sum_{t=1}^{N}\\mathbb E[I_t]=Np_1,\\qquad \\mathbb E[\\widehat p_1]=p_1.\\]\n<p>Equal marginal probabilities suffice for this expectation calculation; independence becomes important for the standard variance and concentration formulas used later. We assume independent trials in the lab below. If the preparation drifts or trials influence one another, an independent, fixed-probability model may be inappropriate.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/classical-information/probability-frequency.svg\" alt=\"A probability model assigns probabilities 0.75 and 0.25 to zero and one. A separate illustrative 100-trial sample has counts 72 and 28, showing that observed frequency can differ from model probability.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 2.</strong> Model probabilities and one illustrative sample describe different quantities. The counts 72 and 28 are a labelled illustration, not a reported experiment or guaranteed result. <span class=\"draft-label\">Draft figure for instructor review.</span></figcaption></figure>\n<div data-lab=\"probability\"></div>\n<p>The simulation is a way to inspect the model, not a proof of a probability law. Repeat a batch with unchanged settings and compare the results. At probabilities 0 and 1 the outcomes are deterministic. At intermediate probabilities different batches will usually have different counts. The more formal treatment of uncertainty comes in Chapter 8.</p>\n<div data-quiz=\"prob-frequency\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"conditioning\"><h2>4. Conditioning: updating a description when information arrives</h2>\n<p>Return to the weighted die. Suppose we learn that the result lies in \\(E=\\{2,4,6\\}\\). Outcomes outside \\(E\\) are now excluded, while the relative weights of the surviving faces remain 2:3:3. To make those weights sum to one, divide by their total probability.</p>\n<p>For \\(\\Pr(E)&gt;0\\), the conditional probability of a face \\(j\\) is</p>\n\\[\\Pr(X=j\\mid E)=\\frac{\\Pr(\\{X=j\\}\\cap E)}{\\Pr(E)}=\\begin{cases}p_j/\\Pr(E),&j\\in E,\\\\0,&j\\notin E.\\end{cases}\\]\n<p>In this example, \\(\\Pr(E)=8/12\\). Hence the conditional probabilities of faces 2, 4, and 6 are \\(2/8,3/8,3/8\\). In the full face order,</p>\n\\[p_{\\mid E}=\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\1/4\\\\0\\\\3/8\\\\0\\\\3/8\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>The denominator is a probability because these entries are classical probabilities. A later quantum conditional-state calculation will divide surviving <em>amplitudes</em> by the square root of an outcome probability. The difference follows from what the entries represent; it is not an arbitrary change of convention.</p>\n<p>If an event has probability zero under our model, this elementary conditional formula is undefined. Dividing by zero does not create a meaningful conditional distribution. Such an observation would require us to reconsider the model or preparation.</p>\n<aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>Observation and physical operation are different ideas.</strong><p>Learning a classical state can update our information to a point mass. That alone does not assert a physical disturbance of the device. If a measurement or intervention changes a system, the physical change needs its own model. We will handle quantum measurement on its own terms later.</p></aside></section>\n<section id=\"practice\"><h2>5. Practice and worked reasoning</h2>\n<ol class=\"exercise-list\"><li><strong>Validity and an event.</strong> Is \\((0.1,0.4,0.5)^{\\mathsf T}\\) a valid distribution on labels (A,B,C)? Find the probability that the outcome is not B.\n<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Check nonnegativity and the total before calculating an event probability.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>All entries are nonnegative and \\(0.1+0.4+0.5=1\\), so it is valid. The event not B contains A and C, giving \\(0.1+0.5=0.6\\), equivalently \\(1-0.4\\).</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Count versus probability.</strong> A fixed model has \\(p_1=0.3\\). In 200 independent trials you observe 68 ones. State the expected count, observed frequency, and what can be concluded about the exact model probability.\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The expected count is \\(200(0.3)=60\\); the observed frequency is \\(68/200=0.34\\). A finite sample can differ from its expectation. This single observation neither proves the exact probability is 0.34 nor, by itself, establishes that the model is wrong. Assessing the size of a discrepancy requires an uncertainty analysis and the sampling assumptions.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Conditional die.</strong> For the distribution in this lesson, condition on \\(A=\\{1,2,3\\}\\). What is the probability of face 2 given A? What if you try to condition on the empty event?\n<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Add the surviving weights before renormalizing them.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>\\(\\Pr(A)=(1+2+1)/12=1/3\\). Therefore \\(\\Pr(X=2\\mid A)=(2/12)/(4/12)=1/2\\). The empty event has probability zero, so the elementary conditional formula is not defined for it.</p></details></li></ol>\n<aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>What to carry forward</strong><p>A distribution is a normalized nonnegative vector. Events collect outcomes, counts summarize repeated trials, and conditioning updates a description using information. Next we will represent operations on these vectors by matrices.</p></aside></section>\n"}, {"id": "classical-operations", "number": "1.3", "title": "Deterministic and stochastic operations", "part": "Part I · Chapter 1", "status": "Full lecture notes · instructor-review draft", "objectives": ["Build a matrix from its response to definite inputs.", "Derive the distribution update p′=Mp and prove normalization preservation.", "Distinguish stochastic validity, invertibility, and allowed reversal."], "body_file": "chapters/classical-information/3-operations.html", "quizzes": [{"id": "operations-columns", "question": "In our column-vector convention, what does column j of an operation matrix describe?", "options": ["The output distribution conditional on input j.", "The input distribution conditional on output j.", "The final distribution for every possible preparation."], "answer": 0, "feedback": ["Correct. Each input basis vector selects its corresponding output column.", "That reverses the conditional direction. The input is the column label.", "A different uncertain preparation combines the columns with different weights."]}, {"id": "operations-inverse", "question": "Reset-to-zero preserves total probability. Must it therefore be reversible for every input?", "options": ["Yes: preservation of probability guarantees an inverse.", "No: distinct inputs are mapped to the same output.", "Only if the input distribution is uniform."], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["Probability preservation is weaker than reversibility. Reset is a counterexample.", "Correct. The common output cannot identify which of the distinct inputs occurred.", "A chosen input distribution does not create an inverse that recovers every input."]}], "notebook": "00-classical-information.ipynb", "notebook_kind": "classical", "source": "Lecture Slides - 1 to 7.pdf, PDF pp.7,11. The total-probability derivation, random-flip calculation and distinction between algebraic and stochastic inverses expand the slide material.", "prerequisites": "Basic arithmetic and elementary algebra. A matrix-multiplication refresher is included before composition.", "body": "<p class=\"lead\">A state description tells us what may be present. An operation tells us how that description changes. By placing an operation's response to each definite input into a matrix, we can calculate its response to an uncertain input.</p>\n<section id=\"deterministic\"><h2>1. Deterministic operations as functions</h2>\n<p>A deterministic operation on a finite state set is a function \\(f:\\Sigma\\to\\Sigma\\): for every definite input label, it specifies one definite output label. “Deterministic” describes the operation's response given its input. It does not require the input to be known to us.</p>\n<p>For a bit, there are four such functions. There are two choices for \\(f(0)\\) and, independently, two choices for \\(f(1)\\), giving \\(2\\times2=4\\) possibilities.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><caption>All deterministic one-bit functions</caption><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>f(0)</th><th>f(1)</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Identity</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>Keep the input</td></tr><tr><td>Flip</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>Exchange 0 and 1</td></tr><tr><td>Reset to 0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>Output 0 for either input</td></tr><tr><td>Reset to 1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>Output 1 for either input</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Define the operation matrix by</p>\n\\[M_{ij}=\\begin{cases}1,&i=f(j),\\\\0,&i\\ne f(j).\\end{cases}\\]\n<p>The <strong>column index is the input</strong> and the row index is the output. Column \\(j\\) is the output basis vector produced by input \\(|j\\rangle\\). Therefore \\(M|j\\rangle=|f(j)\\rangle\\). This rule is safer than memorizing matrices without knowing which direction their indices represent.</p>\n<p>In basis order \\((0,1)\\), the four matrices are</p>\n\\[I=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix},\\quad X=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad R_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad R_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>For example, both columns of \\(R_0\\) are \\((1,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\), because either input is sent to 0. The symbol \\(X\\) here denotes the classical flip matrix; the same matrix will later act on quantum amplitudes, under a different state model.</p></section>\n<section id=\"stochastic\"><h2>2. Stochastic operations: a distribution for each input</h2>\n<p>An operation may have a random response even when its input is definite. Instead of one output label, associate a conditional distribution with each input:</p>\n\\[M_{ij}=\\Pr(\\text{output}=i\\mid\\text{input}=j).\\]\n<p>For every fixed input \\(j\\), these output probabilities must be nonnegative and sum to one. Thus a matrix representing such an operation satisfies</p>\n\\[M_{ij}\\ge0,\\qquad \\sum_i M_{ij}=1\\quad\\text{for every column }j.\\]\n<p>We call this a <strong>column-stochastic matrix</strong>. Some texts use row probability vectors and row-stochastic matrices. Both conventions can work, but we consistently use columns. Swapping conventions halfway through a calculation changes the meaning of the entries.</p>\n<h3>Worked example: a random flip</h3>\n<p>Suppose the operation flips a bit with probability \\(\\varepsilon\\) and leaves it unchanged with probability \\(1-\\varepsilon\\), for either input. Here \\(0\\le\\varepsilon\\le1\\). Its matrix is</p>\n\\[B_{\\varepsilon}=\\begin{pmatrix}1-\\varepsilon&\\varepsilon\\\\\\varepsilon&1-\\varepsilon\\end{pmatrix}=(1-\\varepsilon)I+\\varepsilon X.\\]\n<p>The first column means input 0 stays 0 with probability \\(1-\\varepsilon\\) and becomes 1 with probability \\(\\varepsilon\\). The second column gives the corresponding probabilities for input 1. At \\(\\varepsilon=0\\) the operation is identity; at \\(\\varepsilon=1\\) it is a definite flip. At \\(\\varepsilon=1/2\\), either definite input produces the uniform output distribution.</p>\n<p>Not every stochastic operation treats the inputs symmetrically. The example</p>\n\\[A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1/2\\\\0&1/2\\end{pmatrix}\\]\n<p>leaves input 0 unchanged but, given input 1, outputs 0 or 1 with equal probability. Its columns each sum to one even though its rows do not. This is a useful check that we are applying the correct convention.</p>\n<div data-quiz=\"operations-columns\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"evolution\"><h2>3. Deriving the update rule \\(p'=Mp\\)</h2>\n<p>Let \\(p_j\\) be the probability of input \\(j\\), and let \\(p'_i\\) be the probability of output \\(i\\). To obtain output \\(i\\), the input could have been any label \\(j\\). The probability of each such route is its input probability multiplied by the operation's conditional output probability. Summing these mutually exclusive routes gives the law of total probability:</p>\n\\[p'_i=\\sum_j\\Pr(\\text{output}=i\\mid\\text{input}=j)\\Pr(\\text{input}=j)=\\sum_jM_{ij}p_j.\\]\n<p>This is exactly the definition of the \\(i\\)-th entry of a matrix–vector product. Therefore,</p>\n\\[p'=Mp.\\]\n<p>For a bit, the calculation is explicitly</p>\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix}p'_0\\\\p'_1\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}M_{00}p_0+M_{01}p_1\\\\M_{10}p_0+M_{11}p_1\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<h3>Worked example: uncertain input and uncertain operation</h3>\n<p>Let \\(p=(3/4,1/4)^{\\mathsf T}\\) and \\(\\varepsilon=1/4\\). Then</p>\n\\[B_{1/4}p=\\begin{pmatrix}3/4&1/4\\\\1/4&3/4\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}3/4\\\\1/4\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}9/16+1/16\\\\3/16+3/16\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}5/8\\\\3/8\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>The second output probability has two contributions: start in 0 and flip, or start in 1 and do not flip. This explanation is the probabilistic meaning of the two terms in the second matrix row. We must not apply an operation only to the most likely input and discard the other possibility.</p>\n<div data-lab=\"operations\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"normalization\"><h2>4. Why the output is still a probability distribution</h2>\n<p>We should verify that the update rule respects the definition of a distribution. Each output entry is a sum of products of nonnegative numbers, so \\(p'_i\\ge0\\). For the total probability, interchange the finite sums:</p>\n\\[\\begin{aligned}\\sum_i p'_i&=\\sum_i\\sum_jM_{ij}p_j\\\\&=\\sum_jp_j\\left(\\sum_iM_{ij}\\right)\\\\&=\\sum_jp_j=1.\\end{aligned}\\]\n<p>The third line uses the fact that each column of \\(M\\) sums to one. Thus every valid column-stochastic matrix maps every valid input distribution to another valid distribution. This proof explains why the column condition is the right one; checking a few numerical inputs alone would not establish the general statement.</p>\n<p>A deterministic function is a special stochastic operation: each column has one 1 and all other entries 0. It still acts on uncertain input distributions by the same rule. There are therefore two distinct sources of uncertainty: uncertainty in the input preparation and randomness in the operation. They can occur separately or together.</p></section>\n<section id=\"reversibility\"><h2>5. Preserving probability does not imply reversibility</h2>\n<p>The flip has an inverse: applying it twice restores every definite input, so \\(X^{-1}=X\\). Identity is its own inverse too. Reset-to-zero is different: inputs 0 and 1 both become output 0. Seeing that output does not identify which input occurred.</p>\n<p>There cannot be an inverse operation recovering every reset input, because it would have to send the same vector \\(|0\\rangle\\) to two different vectors. The identical columns of \\(R_0\\) make that loss of input information visible. Reset nevertheless preserves total probability perfectly.</p>\n<p>An invertible numerical matrix need not have an inverse that is a physical stochastic operation. For example,</p>\n\\[B_{1/4}^{-1}=\\begin{pmatrix}3/2&-1/2\\\\-1/2&3/2\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>This is an algebraic inverse, but it contains negative entries. It cannot act on every probability distribution as a stochastic process. Deterministic bijections, represented by permutation matrices, do have stochastic inverses. This distinction prepares us for the stronger restrictions on closed-system quantum operations later.</p>\n<div data-quiz=\"operations-inverse\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"practice\"><h2>6. Practice and worked reasoning</h2>\n<ol class=\"exercise-list\"><li><strong>Build a matrix from words.</strong> An operation leaves 0 unchanged. Given 1, it outputs 0 with probability 1/3 and 1 with probability 2/3. Construct its matrix and apply it to \\(p=(1/4,3/4)^{\\mathsf T}\\).\n<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Fill the first column from input 0 and the second from input 1. Then multiply by the input probabilities.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The matrix is \\(M=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1/3\\\\0&2/3\\end{pmatrix}\\). Therefore \\(Mp=(1/4+(1/3)(3/4),\\ (2/3)(3/4))^{\\mathsf T}=(1/2,1/2)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Both columns sum to one, and the result is normalized.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Check the convention.</strong> Is \\(C=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\1/2&1/2\\end{pmatrix}\\) a valid operation on our column probability vectors?\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>No. The column sums are 3/2 and 1/2. For example, \\(C|0\\rangle=(1,1/2)^{\\mathsf T}\\) is not a normalized distribution. Its rows summing to one does not make it valid under our column convention.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Reversal.</strong> Explain why reset cannot be reversed for every unknown input. Is the algebraic inverse of \\(B_{1/4}\\) a stochastic matrix?\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>Reset maps distinct inputs to the same output, so no function of that output can recover both inputs correctly. The stated inverse of \\(B_{1/4}\\) reverses the matrix algebra but has negative entries, so it is not a stochastic operation on arbitrary distributions.</p></details></li></ol>\n<aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>What to carry forward</strong><p>Read an operation column by column: each column describes a definite input. The total-probability rule gives \\(p'=Mp\\). Normalization preservation, algebraic invertibility, and reversibility by an allowed stochastic operation are separate properties.</p></aside></section>\n"}, {"id": "classical-composition", "number": "1.4", "title": "Composing operations", "part": "Part I · Chapter 1", "status": "Full lecture notes · instructor-review draft", "objectives": ["Translate a temporal sequence into the correct matrix product.", "Show a noncommuting pair and distinguish it from associativity.", "Prove that a compatible product of stochastic matrices is stochastic."], "body_file": "chapters/classical-information/4-composition.html", "quizzes": [{"id": "composition-order", "question": "A acts first, then B, on a column probability vector p. Which expression describes the result?", "options": ["ABp", "BAp", "A+B+p"], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["ABp applies B to p first; it reverses the stated sequence.", "Correct. The intermediate state is Ap, so the second action is B(Ap).", "A temporal composition is matrix multiplication, not addition."]}, {"id": "composition-commute", "question": "Identity and flip agree on the uniform bit distribution. Are they therefore the same operation?", "options": ["Yes: one matching output proves equality.", "No: they disagree on a definite basis input.", "They agree on every other input too."], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["One input is not enough to establish equality of linear maps.", "Correct. On |0⟩, identity outputs |0⟩ while flip outputs |1⟩.", "They agree on the uniform vector, but not on the basis inputs."]}], "notebook": "00-classical-information.ipynb", "notebook_kind": "classical", "source": "Lecture Slides - 1 to 7.pdf, PDF pp.12–13. The multiplication refresher, closure proof, commuting example and state-sufficiency discussion provide expanded lecture-note exposition.", "prerequisites": "Basic arithmetic and elementary algebra. A matrix-multiplication refresher is included before composition.", "body": "<p class=\"lead\">Once a single operation is represented by a matrix, a sequence of operations becomes a matrix product. The order of that product records the order of the process—and reversing the order can change the answer completely.</p>\n<section id=\"refresher\"><h2>1. A short matrix-multiplication refresher</h2>\n<p>A matrix–vector product takes one row of the matrix at a time, multiplies its entries by the matching vector entries, and adds the results. For</p>\n\\[A=\\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad v=\\begin{pmatrix}u\\\\w\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad Av=\\begin{pmatrix}au+bw\\\\cu+dw\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>A matrix–matrix product applies the same rule to each column of the right-hand matrix. If</p>\n\\[B=\\begin{pmatrix}e&f\\\\g&h\\end{pmatrix},\\]\n<p>then</p>\n\\[AB=\\begin{pmatrix}ae+bg&af+bh\\\\ce+dg&cf+dh\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>This is not entry-by-entry multiplication. The \\((i,j)\\) entry is \\((AB)_{ij}=\\sum_k A_{ik}B_{kj}\\). If \\(B\\) maps input states to intermediate states and \\(A\\) maps intermediate states to output states, this sum follows every possible intermediate route.</p>\n<p>Dimensions are part of the meaning. If \\(B\\) maps a \\(d\\)-state input to an \\(r\\)-state intermediate system and \\(A\\) maps that to an \\(m\\)-state output, their dimensions are \\(r\\times d\\) and \\(m\\times r\\). The product \\(AB\\) is \\(m\\times d\\). Its inner dimensions match because the two operations share the same intermediate labels and order. Our lab uses two-state systems, but the rule is more general.</p></section>\n<section id=\"order\"><h2>2. Why the rightmost matrix acts first</h2>\n<p>Let the initial probability vector be \\(p\\). Apply \\(A\\) first, obtaining \\(p^{(1)}=Ap\\). Next apply \\(B\\):</p>\n\\[p^{(2)}=Bp^{(1)}=B(Ap)=(BA)p.\\]\n<p>The last equality uses <strong>associativity</strong>: compatible matrix multiplication can be regrouped without changing its value. It does not permit us to interchange the factors. The operation closest to the input column acts first. Thus the temporal instruction “A, then B” corresponds to \\(BA\\), not \\(AB\\).</p>\n<p>For an ordered sequence \\(M_1,M_2,\\ldots,M_n\\),</p>\n\\[p^{(n)}=M_nM_{n-1}\\cdots M_2M_1p.\\]\n<p>There is no conflict between a process diagram read left to right and a product acting right to left. They are two notations for the same sequence. Write an intermediate state whenever the shorthand becomes confusing.</p>\n<div data-quiz=\"composition-order\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"counterexample\"><h2>3. Worked example: reset and flip do not commute</h2>\n<p>Use the reset-to-zero and flip matrices</p>\n\\[R_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad X=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>Start with any normalized bit distribution \\(p=(p_0,p_1)^{\\mathsf T}\\). If we reset first,</p>\n\\[R_0p=\\begin{pmatrix}p_0+p_1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>Flipping that result gives \\(X(R_0p)=(0,1)^{\\mathsf T}\\): the output is certainly 1. The combined matrix confirms this:</p>\n\\[XR_0=\\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}=R_1.\\]\n<p>Reverse the physical order. Flipping the input first exchanges its probabilities, but resetting afterwards always gives 0:</p>\n\\[R_0(Xp)=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad R_0X=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}=R_0.\\]\n<p>Consequently \\(XR_0\\ne R_0X\\). These operations <strong>do not commute</strong>. The difference is not a rounding error or a matter of which input was most probable: the two output distributions are different for every normalized input.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/classical-information/composition-order.svg\" alt=\"Reset to zero followed by flip always outputs one and has product X R0. Flip followed by reset always outputs zero and has product R0 X.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 3.</strong> Temporal order and matrix order describe the same calculation. Associativity allows regrouping; it does not allow exchanging reset and flip. <span class=\"draft-label\">Draft figure for instructor review.</span></figcaption></figure>\n<div data-lab=\"composition\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"commuting\"><h2>4. Some operations do commute</h2>\n<p>“Matrix multiplication is not commutative” means we cannot assume \\(AB=BA\\) for arbitrary matrices. It does not mean no pair ever commutes. Identity commutes with every compatible square matrix. Any matrix commutes with itself. Less trivially, a random-flip matrix \\(B_\\varepsilon=(1-\\varepsilon)I+\\varepsilon X\\) commutes with \\(X\\):</p>\n\\[\\begin{aligned}XB_\\varepsilon&=(1-\\varepsilon)X+\\varepsilon X^2=(1-\\varepsilon)X+\\varepsilon I,\\\\B_\\varepsilon X&=(1-\\varepsilon)X+\\varepsilon X^2=(1-\\varepsilon)X+\\varepsilon I.\\end{aligned}\\]\n<p>We used \\(X^2=I\\), which simply says that two definite bit flips undo one another. Equality of outputs for one chosen input, however, is not enough to prove equality of two operation matrices. They might agree on that input and disagree on another. Equality as linear maps can be checked on an entire basis, since every vector is a linear combination of basis vectors.</p>\n<div data-quiz=\"composition-commute\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"closure\"><h2>5. A composition is still a valid stochastic operation</h2>\n<p>Let \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) be compatible column-stochastic matrices. The combined operation is \\(C=BA\\), whose entries are</p>\n\\[C_{ij}=\\sum_k B_{ik}A_{kj}.\\]\n<p>Every entry is nonnegative because each term is a product of nonnegative entries. For a fixed input column \\(j\\),</p>\n\\[\\begin{aligned}\\sum_i C_{ij}&=\\sum_i\\sum_k B_{ik}A_{kj}\\\\&=\\sum_k A_{kj}\\left(\\sum_iB_{ik}\\right)\\\\&=\\sum_k A_{kj}=1.\\end{aligned}\\]\n<p>The penultimate equality uses the column sum of \\(B\\); the final equality uses the column sum of \\(A\\). Therefore \\(BA\\) is column-stochastic. By applying this argument repeatedly, any finite sequence of valid stochastic operations remains a valid stochastic operation.</p>\n<p>The probabilistic interpretation is also useful. The index \\(k\\) ranges over the intermediate state. The first operation sends input \\(j\\) to \\(k\\) with probability \\(A_{kj}\\), and the second sends \\(k\\) to output \\(i\\) with probability \\(B_{ik}\\). Multiplying the route probabilities and summing over intermediate labels gives the composite transition rule.</p>\n<aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>A modelling assumption behind the product</strong><p>The same second-operation rule \\(B\\) must apply given the intermediate state. If the second device also depends on hidden history, shared randomness, or a changing environment not represented in that state, the state description may need to be enlarged. A convenient matrix product does not eliminate the need for a faithful model.</p></aside></section>\n<section id=\"practice\"><h2>6. Practice and worked reasoning</h2>\n<ol class=\"exercise-list\"><li><strong>A two-stage calculation.</strong> Begin with \\(p=(3/4,1/4)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Apply a random flip with probability 1/4, then a definite flip. Find the intermediate vector, final vector, and combined matrix.\n<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Write the intermediate result first. The matrix for “random flip, then flip” is \\(XB_{1/4}\\).</p></details>\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The intermediate result is \\((5/8,3/8)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Flipping gives \\((3/8,5/8)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Multiplication gives \\(XB_{1/4}=\\begin{pmatrix}1/4&3/4\\\\3/4&1/4\\end{pmatrix}\\), and applying it directly to the input yields the same final vector.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>One input is not a proof.</strong> Identity and flip both leave the uniform distribution \\((1/2,1/2)^{\\mathsf T}\\) unchanged. Are they the same operation?\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>No. On input \\(|0\\rangle\\), identity outputs \\(|0\\rangle\\) while flip outputs \\(|1\\rangle\\). Agreement on one vector is weaker than agreement as operators. Checking their action on both bit basis vectors would distinguish them.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Three operations.</strong> A process applies \\(A\\), then \\(B\\), then \\(C\\). Write the combined matrix. If all are column-stochastic, explain why their product is valid.\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The combined matrix is \\(CBA\\). The closure proof shows \\(BA\\) is column-stochastic, and applying it again shows \\(C(BA)\\) is column-stochastic. Associativity allows the grouping; no factor has been interchanged.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>What history must be modelled?</strong> A second device behaves differently depending on the original input, even when the same intermediate bit reaches it. Can a single matrix conditioned only on the intermediate bit describe both cases?\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>Not in general. If the same represented intermediate state leads to different conditional output rules depending on omitted history, that bit alone is not a sufficient state description for the proposed composition model. Include the relevant history/environment label or explicitly specify a different operation for each context.</p></details></li></ol></section>\n<section id=\"readiness\"><h2>7. Chapter checkpoint and the road to quantum computing</h2>\n<p>You now have a complete classical calculation pattern: declare the state labels and order; write a probability vector; represent conditional transitions by stochastic columns; compose the matrices in temporal order; interpret the resulting distribution. You should be able to explain each step without relying on a simulation.</p>\n<ul><li>Represent a definite state and an uncertain description in the same declared basis.</li><li>Distinguish a probability, an empirical frequency, and a conditional probability.</li><li>Build an operation from its response to every definite input.</li><li>Derive \\(p'=Mp\\) and explain why normalization is preserved.</li><li>Track intermediate states and show an explicit noncommuting example.</li></ul>\n<p>The next chapter develops complex numbers, inner products, and the rest of our mathematical language. Quantum states will later be described by complex amplitudes, and closed-system gates by unitary matrices. Matrix composition will still matter, but the allowed states, operations, and measurement rules will change. The classical foundation gives us something precise to compare those new rules against.</p>\n</section>\n"}], "legacyLessons": [{"id": "states", "title": "States, amplitudes & measurement", "part": "01 · Quantum foundations", "status": "Draft lesson", "time": "25 min", "source": "Lecture Slides - 1 to 7.pdf, PDF pages 14–20; Practice Problems Q1–5, Q10; introductory transcript used for teaching context.", "objectives": ["Normalize a complex state vector.", "Distinguish amplitude from probability.", "Explain why phase can matter even when Z-basis probabilities do not change."], "body": "<h2>From probabilities to amplitudes</h2><p>A classical random bit is described by probabilities that sum to one. A pure qubit is described by two complex amplitudes: <strong>|ψ⟩ = α|0⟩ + β|1⟩</strong>. Their squared magnitudes sum to one: |α|² + |β|² = 1.</p><p>Computational-basis measurement returns 0 with probability |α|² and 1 with probability |β|². One measurement gives one classical outcome; it does not reveal both amplitudes.</p><h2>A worked example</h2><p>For |ψ⟩ = [|0⟩ + (1 + 3i)|1⟩]/√11, the squared magnitudes are 1/11 and (1² + 3²)/11 = 10/11. They add to one, so the state is normalized. This works through practice question Q1.</p><aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>Keep this distinction</strong><p>Amplitudes can interfere. Probabilities are real, nonnegative numbers obtained after taking squared magnitudes.</p></aside><h2>Explore a pure qubit</h2><p>Up to global phase, |ψ⟩ = cos(θ/2)|0⟩ + exp(iφ) sin(θ/2)|1⟩. The Bloch coordinates are (sin θ cos φ, sin θ sin φ, cos θ). Change φ while holding θ fixed. Predict which measurement probabilities will change.</p>", "lab": "bloch", "quiz": {"question": "With θ = π/2, changing φ from 0 to π changes which quantity?", "options": ["The Z-basis probability of 1", "The X-basis probability of +", "The normalization"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Z-basis probabilities remain 1/2. The state changes from |+⟩ to |−⟩, so the X-basis probability of + changes from 1 to 0."}, "exercise": "Solve Q2–5 and Q10 in your practice sheet. For Q2, use |1−i|² = 2 before normalizing.", "solution": "Q2: N = 1/√6 and vector (1−i, 2)ᵀ/√6. Q10: θ=π/2, φ=π gives |−⟩.", "notebook": "01-qubit-foundations.ipynb"}, {"id": "gates", "title": "Gates, phase & interference", "part": "01 · Quantum foundations", "status": "Draft lesson", "time": "25 min", "source": "Lecture Slides - 1 to 7.pdf, PDF pages 21–28 and 49–54; Practice Problems Q6–9.", "objectives": ["Apply gates in circuit order.", "Convert relative phase into measurement statistics.", "Recognize global-phase equivalence."], "body": "<h2>Operations preserve normalization</h2><p>A closed-system quantum gate is unitary: U†U = I. The Hadamard gate maps |0⟩ to |+⟩ and |1⟩ to |−⟩. The Z gate leaves |0⟩ unchanged and negates the amplitude of |1⟩.</p><h2>Work through H → Z → H</h2><p>Start in |0⟩. The first H prepares (|0⟩+|1⟩)/√2. Z changes this to (|0⟩−|1⟩)/√2. The final H gives |1⟩. The phase difference becomes a deterministic measurement outcome. Matrix multiplication writes the same sequence as HZH|0⟩, with the rightmost operator acting first.</p><aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>Same probabilities, different states</strong><p>|+⟩ and |−⟩ have identical computational-basis measurement probabilities. Applying H before measurement distinguishes them.</p></aside><h2>Use the existing gate lab</h2><p>Try Pauli Z on |+⟩, then compare with Z on |0⟩. Predict the end point before playing each rotation. The supplied animation is retained as a separate lab.</p><p><a href=\"animations/pauli_gate_bloch_sphere_animation.html\">Open Pauli gate / Bloch sphere lab ↗</a></p>", "lab": null, "quiz": {"question": "What is the result of H → Z → H applied to |0⟩?", "options": ["|0⟩", "|1⟩", "A 50/50 Z-basis distribution"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "H prepares |+⟩, Z turns it into |−⟩, and H maps |−⟩ to |1⟩."}, "exercise": "Solve Q6–9. Compare XZ|ψ⟩ and ZX|ψ⟩: do their different signs imply distinguishable physical states?", "solution": "XZ = −ZX. For any input the outputs differ by global phase −1, so they give identical statistics for all measurements.", "notebook": "01-qubit-foundations.ipynb"}, {"id": "entanglement", "title": "Two qubits & entanglement", "part": "01 · Quantum foundations", "status": "Draft lesson", "time": "30 min", "source": "Lecture Slides - 1 to 7.pdf, PDF pages 29–47; Practice Problems Q11–25.", "objectives": ["Expand tensor products in a fixed basis order.", "Test pure two-qubit states for separability.", "Prepare and measure a Bell state in Qiskit."], "body": "<h2>Two systems, one state</h2><p>We use basis order |00⟩, |01⟩, |10⟩, |11⟩. A two-qubit pure state is a|00⟩ + b|01⟩ + c|10⟩ + d|11⟩. Product states factor into two single-qubit vectors. For a nonzero normalized pure state, factorization is possible exactly when ad − bc = 0.</p><h2>Build a Bell state</h2><p>Begin at |00⟩. Apply H to the left qubit, giving (|00⟩+|10⟩)/√2. A CNOT controlled by that qubit gives (|00⟩+|11⟩)/√2. Here ad−bc=1/2, so the state is entangled.</p><p>Each individual bit is random in computational-basis measurement, but their outcomes always agree. Correlations in one measurement basis alone are not proof of entanglement: a classical mixture of 00 and 11 has those correlations too.</p><aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>Watch the wire order</strong><p>Qiskit displays q₀ at the top, but a two-qubit ket is written |q₁q₀⟩. To implement the left-qubit Bell construction above, use h(1), then cx(1, 0).</p></aside><h2>Partial measurement</h2><p>Project onto the observed outcome and divide surviving amplitudes by the square root of its probability. For a Bell state, observing left bit 0 leaves |00⟩ and observing 1 leaves |11⟩. A zero-probability outcome has no conditional state.</p><p><a href=\"animations/quantum_computing_interactive_companion.html\">Open tensor, measurement & Bell-state companion ↗</a></p>", "lab": null, "quiz": {"question": "For a normalized pure state with coefficients a,b,c,d, when is it separable?", "options": ["a+b+c+d = 1", "ad−bc = 0", "All four probabilities are equal"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The 2×2 coefficient matrix has rank one exactly when its determinant ad−bc vanishes. This criterion applies to pure two-qubit states."}, "exercise": "Solve Q15, Q16, Q20 and Q25. Then compare Bell-state measurements in Z and X bases in the notebook.", "solution": "Q16: P(left=0)=1/2 and P(left=1)=1/2. Conditional full states are (|00⟩+|01⟩)/√2 and |11⟩. Q25: q₀=1, q₁=0 means |01⟩.", "notebook": "02-bell-states.ipynb"}, {"id": "classifier", "title": "A first variational classifier", "part": "04 · Learning with circuits", "status": "Draft lesson", "time": "35 min", "source": "Lecture 10 Building a Quantum Classifier 1.pdf, PDF pages 17–22, 31 and 33–42. Supplemental one-qubit example.", "objectives": ["Separate data encoding from trainable parameters.", "Read a probability as a model prediction.", "Compute an expectation gradient by parameter shift."], "body": "<h2>A quantum model inside a classical loop</h2><p>Encode a scalar feature x with Rᵧ(x), apply a trainable Rᵧ(w), then measure. Starting from |0⟩, the probability of label 1 is p(1|x,w) = sin²((x+w)/2). A classical optimizer updates w to reduce a loss over training examples.</p><p>This tiny model combines the two rotations into one. It is an intentionally limited teaching model that a classical computer simulates easily. It demonstrates the training loop and makes no claim of quantum advantage.</p><h2>From probability to expectation</h2><p>The Z expectation is f(x,w)=1−2p(1|x,w)=cos(x+w). For this rotation, ∂f/∂w = [f(x,w+π/2)−f(x,w−π/2)]/2. A loss gradient still needs the chain rule; shifting the whole loss is not generally the same calculation.</p><aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>Evaluate the complete experiment</strong><p>Fit preprocessing on training data only. Keep a test set for final evaluation. Compare classical baselines and report circuit depth, sampling cost, seeds, and uncertainty.</p></aside><h2>Explore before training</h2><p>Move x and w to predict the circuit output. The notebook implements Qiskit statevector predictions, parameter-shift gradients, a small training loop, and a held-out comparison.</p>", "lab": "classifier", "quiz": {"question": "For x=0 and w=π, what is p(1)?", "options": ["0", "1/2", "1"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Rᵧ(π)|0⟩ = |1⟩, so sin²(π/2)=1."}, "exercise": "Derive ∂p(1|x,w)/∂w. Explain why changing the number of shots changes uncertainty but not the ideal probability.", "solution": "∂p/∂w = sin(x+w)/2. For N independent shots the estimated probability has variance p(1−p)/N.", "notebook": "03-variational-classifier.ipynb"}], "planned": [{"title": "Chapter 2 · Mathematical language", "needs": "Complex numbers, inner products, Dirac notation, and matrix operations."}, {"title": "Chapter 3 · Qubits and measurement", "needs": "Pure states, Born probabilities, phase, and conditional projection."}, {"title": "Chapter 4 · Gates, interference and measurement bases", "needs": "Unitary evolution, gates, rotations, and Bloch geometry."}, {"title": "Chapter 5 · Composite systems and entanglement", "needs": "Tensor products, controlled gates, Bell states, and partial measurement."}, {"title": "Chapter 6 · Registers, conventions and oracles", "needs": "Circuit/readout ordering and reversible oracle access."}, {"title": "Chapter 7 · Learning from quantum algorithms", "needs": "Kickback, Deutsch, Deutsch–Jozsa, and Simon’s sampled constraints."}, {"title": "Chapter 8 · Observables, statistics and noisy devices", "needs": "Expectations, uncertainty, mixtures, reduced states, and simple noise models."}, {"title": "Part II · Classical machine learning bridge", "needs": "Learning loops, optimization, feature spaces, kernels, and generalization."}, {"title": "Part III · Quantum machine learning", "needs": "Encoding, variational classifiers, quantum kernels, and responsible evaluation."}]};
